📘 TOPIC SHEET 7: Security (Roles, Teams, Field-Level, Hierarchy)
(~12 questions — complex combinations) ⚠️
________________________________________
📖 Concept
Security Layers (from broadest to narrowest)
Layer 1: ENVIRONMENT ACCESS
    → Who can even SEE the environment?
    → Controlled by: Office 365 / Azure AD Security Groups

Layer 2: SECURITY ROLES
    → What can users DO inside the environment?
    → CRUD privileges on tables at different access levels

Layer 3: TEAMS
    → How do users SHARE access to records?
    → Owner Teams vs Access Teams

Layer 4: FIELD-LEVEL SECURITY
    → Which specific FIELDS can users see/edit?
    → Field Security Profiles

Layer 5: HIERARCHY SECURITY
    → Can managers see subordinates' records?
    → Manager Hierarchy vs Position Hierarchy
Security Roles — Access Levels
Access Level	Symbol	What They See
None	○ (empty)	No access
User	🟡 (one circle)	Own records only
Business Unit	🟢 (two circles)	Own BU records
Parent: Child BU	🔵 (three circles)	Own BU + child BU records
Organization	🔴 (four circles)	ALL records in org
Teams Comparison
Feature	Owner Team	Access Team
Can own records	✅ Yes	❌ No
Has security roles	✅ Yes	❌ No (inherits from sharing)
Created how	Manually by admin	Dynamically (auto or manual)
Best for	Permanent groups (Sales Team, Support Team)	Temporary/dynamic access to specific records
Members	Fixed membership	Flexible, record-by-record
Field-Level Security
Step	What To Do
1	Enable column for field-level security (column properties → "Enable column security")
2	Create a Field Security Profile
3	Add users/teams to the profile
4	Set Read/Create/Update permissions per field in the profile
________________________________________
🔑 Rules to Memorize
Rule	Remember
1	Environment access = Office 365 Security Group (restricts who sees the environment)
2	Security roles = CRUD privileges at different access levels
3	Owner teams = own records + have security roles
4	Access teams = temporary access, don't own records, no security roles
5	Field-level security = controls individual FIELD access (not record, not table)
6	Field Security Profile = the container for field permissions
7	Manager Hierarchy = managers auto-see subordinates' records (depth configurable)
8	Position Hierarchy = based on position, not direct reporting line
9	Conditional Access (Azure AD) = restrict by location, device, IP
10	User/Team owned table = supports security roles, sharing, teams
11	Organization-owned table = no record-level security, everyone sees all
12	Environment Maker = can create apps/flows but NOT access data
13	Basic User (Common Data Service User) = can run apps + access own data
________________________________________
📝 All Related Questions + Answers
Q12, Q16, Q253-254: Restrict environment access
Scenario: Two environments — users must NOT see the other one
Answer: ✅ Office 365 / Azure AD Security Group
How: Associate a security group with the environment → only members see it
❌ Security roles — controls what users do INSIDE, not whether they SEE the environment
❌ Privileges — too granular
❌ App security — controls app access, not environment visibility
________________________________________
Q30, Q47: Security role for canvas app user (no extra permissions)
Scenario: User has Dataverse access, needs to create canvas apps, least privilege
Answer: ✅ Environment Maker (Q30) or Basic User (Q47 — depends on exact wording)
•	Environment Maker = create apps/flows (but not access data)
•	Basic User = run apps + access own data
❌ Environment Admin — too much privilege
❌ System Customizer — can customize but overkill for just creating canvas apps
________________________________________
Q44-45: Restrict canvas app by location
Scenario: Employees must access app only from specific locations
•	Ensure access from specific region → Azure Active Directory (Conditional Access)
•	Specify allowed locations → Conditional Access Policy (named locations)
❌ Canvas app settings — no location restriction setting
❌ Security roles — no location-based control
________________________________________
Q48-49: Owner Teams vs Access Teams
Requirements:
•	Team can own records → Owner Team
•	Team membership is auto-managed by Azure AD group → AAD Group Team
•	Temporary access to specific records without ownership → Access Team
•	Different access rights per record → Access Team
Key: Own records = Owner Team. Dynamic/temporary = Access Team
________________________________________
Q51: Manager Hierarchy issues
Scenario: CEO sees all, managers see their reports, but reps need to see each other's data across managers
Fix: Give support representatives a security role with Organization-level Read on customer table
Why: Hierarchy alone won't give cross-manager visibility — need explicit security role
________________________________________
Q55: Security roles at scale (5000 users)
•	Assign roles based on teams → Use Owner Teams with security roles attached
•	Minimize admin effort → Team-based security role assignment (not individual)
Key: At scale, assign security roles to TEAMS, not individual users
________________________________________
Q62: Field-Level Security for sales associates (view only)
•	Enable fields for field-level security → Power Apps app designer (column properties)
•	Set security settings to view only → Field Security Profiles
Steps: Enable column security → Create profile → Add sales associates → Set Read=Yes, Update=No
________________________________________
Q69: Hide address columns from users
Answer: ✅ C. Use column-level security to remove read access
❌ Delete columns — destroys data
❌ Disable search — hides from search, not from views
❌ Business rules — can hide on forms but not from views/filters
________________________________________
Q259-260: Access teams for service requests
Scenario: After service request completed, only QV team should access records
•	During work → Access team (add team member for temporary access)
•	After completion → Assign records to QV team (owner team)
Key: Access teams = temporary. Owner teams = permanent ownership
________________________________________
